<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Isabella II of Jerusalem</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1212">1212</a>–<a href="/years/1228">1228</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Yolande of Brienne</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Isabella II, also called Yolande of Brienne, was queen of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> from 1212 to 1228, inheriting the crown as an infant from her mother Maria of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montferrat">Montferrat</a>. In 1225 she married Emperor Frederick II, who took the royal title, and she died in 1228 shortly after giving birth to their son Conrad. Through her the kingdom's crown passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hohenstaufen">Hohenstaufen dynasty</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/isabella-ii-of-jerusalem.jpg" alt="Isabella II of Jerusalem" width="159" height="211" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Giovanni Villani, Chronica, 14. Jahrhundert, Biblioteca Apostolica Vaticana, Rom Cod. Chigi L VIII 296, fol. 74r. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Isabella_of_Brienne_(cropped).JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Isabella II was born in late 1212 in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-jerusalem">Kingdom of Jerusalem</a>, then a rump state without the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>. She was the only child of Queen Maria of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montferrat">Montferrat</a>, who died of complications of childbirth, and so inherited the kingdom within days. The situation had no precedent, because her father, John of Brienne, had become king by marrying Maria and expected to keep the crown for life. A faction around the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-ibelin">Ibelin family</a> held that he could rule only as regent for his daughter. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-innocent-iii">Pope Innocent III</a> promised support to John and Isabella.</p><p>In 1223, at a conference in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferentino">Ferentino</a>, Hermann of Salza, grand master of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Knights</a>, proposed her as a bride for Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-ii">Frederick II</a>, recently widowed and preparing to lead a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a>. John, who wanted a powerful leader to summon Western aid, accepted once Hermann promised him the government of the kingdom for life.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In August 1225 Isabella was married by proxy at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acre">Acre</a> and crowned queen of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyre">Tyre</a>. She sailed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brindisi">Brindisi</a>, where she married Frederick in person on 9 November 1225, becoming empress and queen of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>. Frederick then summoned John and declared that he would take his wife's rights and the kingdom, in breach of his agreement. The leading men who had come with her accepted him as king. The pope refused to call him king of Jerusalem for six years.</p><p>Frederick kept Isabella in relative seclusion at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palermo">Palermo</a> and did not take her on his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a> in 1227. Chroniclers close to her father said he mistreated her; the historians Guy Perry and David Abulafia doubt such stories. Isabella issued a charter in which she is principal grantor, the first by a queen of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> since Melisende, and her consent appears in another. A daughter, Margaret, born in November 1226, died in August 1227.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Isabella gave birth to her son Conrad II on 25 April 1228 and died a few days later at the age of fifteen. She was buried in the Cathedral of Saint Peter in Andria. Rumours that Frederick had killed her, probably encouraged by her father, are dismissed by Abulafia as nonsense. Through her marriage the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-jerusalem">Kingdom of Jerusalem</a> passed to largely absentee kings until its fall sixty-six years later, and Perry argues that she simply did not live long enough to have much impact.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Isabella II of Jerusalem” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The crown passed several times through women: Melisende, daughter of Baldwin II, ruled with her husband Fulk of Anjou from 1131 and remained powerful after his death, and the claims of Sibylla, Isabella I and <b>Isabella II</b> brought in husbands from the West.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-jerusalem">Kingdom of Jerusalem · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Acre, in what is now <a href="/countries/israel">Israel</a>. Died in Andria, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=32.93,35.08&amp;year=1222" aria-label="Acre on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5449 968 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Isabella II of Jerusalem"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5899 1238h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5899 1238h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=32.93,35.08&amp;year=1222">What was here in 1222? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
